1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,787
Level one, negative $412.

2
00:00:04,730 --> 00:00:09,221
It starts with a bottle of laundry detergent. $3.79.

3
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:13,561
You buy it at 11:40 on a Tuesday night at the corner store

4
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:18,348
next to the laundromat, because the machines eat quarters and you forgot soap.

5
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:23,746
You tap your card without thinking. The card reader beeps. Approved.

6
00:00:24,150 --> 00:00:27,303
That's the cruel part. It always says approved.

7
00:00:28,090 --> 00:00:33,900
The next morning there's a text from your bank. Overdraft fee. $35.

8
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:39,497
For $3.79 of soap. By Friday, two more charges clear behind it,

9
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:43,354
a $9 phone app and a $14 tank of gas,

10
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:50,537
and each one gets its own $35. Your balance reads negative $412.

11
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:55,778
You didn't spend $412. You were charged for being short.

12
00:00:56,570 --> 00:01:01,087
You call the bank on your lunch break. Forty-one minutes of hold music.

13
00:01:01,490 --> 00:01:04,405
The same eight bars of piano, over and over.

14
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:07,882
Then a woman who reads you a policy in a voice like a

15
00:01:07,950 --> 00:01:10,415
parking meter. She can't reverse it.

16
00:01:10,820 --> 00:01:14,811
She can offer a courtesy review in five to seven business days.

17
00:01:15,210 --> 00:01:18,178
You thank her. You don't know why you thank her.

18
00:01:18,970 --> 00:01:23,482
Sunday, you drive to Nana's. The screen door bangs twice behind you,

19
00:01:23,550 --> 00:01:28,263
the way it has since you were six. Everybody's there. Uncle Pete.

20
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:33,389
Your sister Renée. Your parents. Two cousins and a casserole.

21
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:38,111
And here's the thing about this level. Everyone tells you everything.

22
00:01:38,510 --> 00:01:40,552
Pete tells you to get a second job.

23
00:01:40,950 --> 00:01:46,502
Renée tells you about her raise, $4,000, then the apartment she's eyeing,

24
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:50,355
then the guy she's seeing. Your mother tells you banks are a racket.

25
00:01:50,750 --> 00:01:55,096
Your father tells you, "You need a plan," three times between the salad

26
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:56,002
and the ham.

27
00:01:56,790 --> 00:01:59,352
Nobody tells you the one thing that matters.

28
00:01:59,750 --> 00:02:02,115
Your father's hours got cut in March.

29
00:02:02,510 --> 00:02:04,979
Thirty-two a week now, down from forty.

30
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:08,930
You find out in August, from an envelope on the counter.

31
00:02:09,330 --> 00:02:12,845
When you ask him, he folds it in half and says, "We're fine."

32
00:02:13,630 --> 00:02:16,348
Before you leave, Nana walks you to the car.

33
00:02:16,750 --> 00:02:19,418
She presses something into your jacket pocket.

34
00:02:19,820 --> 00:02:21,825
You don't look until you're on the highway.

35
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:25,565
Two twenties, folded into quarters, soft as cloth.

36
00:02:25,970 --> 00:02:28,630
From the blue cookie tin on top of her fridge.

37
00:02:29,030 --> 00:02:31,307
The one that has never once held cookies.

38
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:34,424
Not charity. Just Nana.

39
00:02:35,210 --> 00:02:36,229
The first rule.

40
00:02:36,630 --> 00:02:42,935
At negative $412, your family tells you everything except what they're carrying.

41
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:46,301
They don't hide it from you. They hide it for you.

42
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:50,425
Level two, $1,900.

43
00:02:51,370 --> 00:02:54,353
Eleven months later, the minus sign is gone.

44
00:02:54,750 --> 00:02:57,748
You work nights at a parcel sorting center now,

45
00:02:57,810 --> 00:03:00,471
six in the evening to two-thirty in the morning,

46
00:03:00,530 --> 00:03:03,391
the conveyor humming so loud you hear it in your sleep.

47
00:03:03,790 --> 00:03:07,234
In the mornings you teach yourself to code on a laptop with a

48
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:10,769
cracked hinge. $1,900 in checking.

49
00:03:11,170 --> 00:03:14,194
You look at it the way you'd look at a candle in wind.

50
00:03:14,980 --> 00:03:19,070
The bank notices before your family does. A new email.

51
00:03:19,470 --> 00:03:24,766
"You may be eligible for overdraft protection." Now. Now they offer it.

52
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:29,603
At the pharmacy counter, picking up your mother's blood pressure pills,

53
00:03:29,670 --> 00:03:33,967
the tech rings up $62 and your card goes through on the first

54
00:03:34,030 --> 00:03:41,269
try. You almost laugh. She doesn't look up. Nobody notices $1,900.

55
00:03:41,670 --> 00:03:42,814
That's the gift of it.

56
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:47,239
At Nana's, Pete still gives you advice. But it's shorter.

57
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:49,711
Renée still tells you about her week.

58
00:03:50,110 --> 00:03:53,606
But now she asks about yours, and waits for the answer.

59
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:59,158
Your father says, "You're doing better." Not a question. A test.

60
00:03:59,950 --> 00:04:04,231
You offer to cover the electric bill. $180.

61
00:04:04,630 --> 00:04:08,774
He looks at you like you said something rude. "We're fine," he says.

62
00:04:09,170 --> 00:04:11,019
Then, quieter, "Keep yours."

63
00:04:11,810 --> 00:04:16,051
Here's what nobody tells you. The first time you have a little extra,

64
00:04:16,110 --> 00:04:19,954
the people who raised you get very careful about not needing it.

65
00:04:20,350 --> 00:04:22,518
Not pride. Protection.

66
00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:26,559
They're guarding the version of you that just got up off the floor.

67
00:04:27,350 --> 00:04:31,669
Nana walks you to the car again. Her hand drifts toward your pocket.

68
00:04:32,070 --> 00:04:34,513
You catch it. You shake your head.

69
00:04:34,910 --> 00:04:38,683
She looks at you for a long second, then pats your cheek instead.

70
00:04:39,470 --> 00:04:44,757
The second rule. At $1,900, they stop telling you what they need.

71
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:46,848
Because you might actually give it.

72
00:04:48,530 --> 00:04:51,071
Level three, $22,000.

73
00:04:52,020 --> 00:04:56,291
The code turns into something. A scheduling tool for home care aides,

74
00:04:56,350 --> 00:05:00,473
the people who drive between six houses a day and keep their shifts

75
00:05:00,540 --> 00:05:03,721
on paper. You built it at a kitchen table.

76
00:05:04,120 --> 00:05:07,268
Fourteen agencies pay you $90 a month.

77
00:05:07,670 --> 00:05:09,945
You quit the sorting center on a Thursday.

78
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:13,890
The quiet in your ears that night is so loud you can't sleep.

79
00:05:14,680 --> 00:05:16,407
$22,000.

80
00:05:16,810 --> 00:05:20,130
You sit down with a loan officer at a credit union about a

81
00:05:20,190 --> 00:05:21,527
small business line.

82
00:05:21,930 --> 00:05:25,323
He has a dish of mints and a framed photo of a fishing

83
00:05:25,390 --> 00:05:29,647
trip. He reads your balance and his whole posture changes,

84
00:05:29,710 --> 00:05:33,766
like someone adjusted his chair. He uses your first name.

85
00:05:34,170 --> 00:05:35,916
He says, "We like to see this."

86
00:05:36,320 --> 00:05:39,371
Two years ago, it was forty-one minutes of piano.

87
00:05:39,770 --> 00:05:42,922
Today, he writes his cell number on the back of a card.

88
00:05:43,710 --> 00:05:45,694
At Nana's, something small shifts.

89
00:05:46,090 --> 00:05:52,190
When Renée shows off new boots, Pete asks her what she paid. $140.

90
00:05:52,590 --> 00:05:56,817
She tells him. When you show up in a new coat, nobody asks.

91
00:05:57,220 --> 00:05:58,067
Not once.

92
00:05:58,860 --> 00:06:01,785
You notice that. You notice everything now.

93
00:06:02,570 --> 00:06:05,310
Later, Renée corners you by the coffee maker.

94
00:06:05,710 --> 00:06:09,755
She's moving in with the guy. She says the deposit is "a lot."

95
00:06:10,150 --> 00:06:14,828
She doesn't say how much. You ask. She says, "Don't worry about it."

96
00:06:15,230 --> 00:06:18,845
Then she tells you anyway. $2,400.

97
00:06:19,240 --> 00:06:21,678
You send it from the bathroom before dessert.

98
00:06:22,470 --> 00:06:26,912
She texts you a heart. Just a heart. In the family group chat,

99
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:30,697
the one called The Porch, she posts a photo of new keys,

100
00:06:30,760 --> 00:06:37,486
and everyone says congratulations. Nobody knows. You don't tell them. Neither does she.

101
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:43,585
The third rule. At $22,000, they stop telling you prices.

102
00:06:43,980 --> 00:06:46,166
Prices are for people who might pay.

103
00:06:47,850 --> 00:06:51,039
Level four, $140,000.

104
00:06:51,990 --> 00:06:55,108
Two hundred agencies. Nine employees.

105
00:06:55,510 --> 00:06:59,530
An office above a dry cleaner that smells like steam and starch.

106
00:06:59,930 --> 00:07:05,660
$140,000 in the business account and a salary you set yourself,

107
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:07,603
which is a strange thing to type.

108
00:07:08,390 --> 00:07:11,575
At the car dealership, the salesman sees the trade-in,

109
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:15,816
then the pre-approval, then you. He brings coffee in a real mug.

110
00:07:16,220 --> 00:07:17,129
Not paper.

111
00:07:17,530 --> 00:07:21,841
He stops saying "this one's popular" and starts saying "this one's quiet."

112
00:07:22,630 --> 00:07:27,690
The bank stops emailing. A person calls instead. Her name is Dana.

113
00:07:28,090 --> 00:07:30,386
She has a direct line and a calm voice,

114
00:07:30,450 --> 00:07:32,596
and she has never once put you on hold.

115
00:07:33,390 --> 00:07:37,962
Then your father's truck needs a transmission. $3,800.

116
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:41,869
You hear about it from Pete, in the driveway, by accident.

117
00:07:42,270 --> 00:07:46,156
You walk inside and offer. Your father says, "We're fine."

118
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:48,166
This time you don't argue.

119
00:07:48,570 --> 00:07:50,892
You just call the shop on Monday and pay it.

120
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:55,778
He doesn't bring it up. Not that week. Not the next.

121
00:07:56,180 --> 00:07:59,481
At Sunday dinner he's polite with you. Careful.

122
00:07:59,880 --> 00:08:02,017
Like you're a guest who might break something.

123
00:08:02,810 --> 00:08:06,162
Your mother says, "He's not mad." She pauses.

124
00:08:06,560 --> 00:08:09,776
"He just doesn't know what he's allowed to say no to anymore."

125
00:08:10,570 --> 00:08:12,599
But here's the part that changes you.

126
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,745
The day you can fix anything, people stop arguing with you.

127
00:08:17,140 --> 00:08:20,724
Pete used to tell you your ideas were dumb. Loudly.

128
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:24,282
With his mouth full. He hasn't said it in a year.

129
00:08:24,680 --> 00:08:27,609
You didn't notice the silence until it was total.

130
00:08:28,400 --> 00:08:33,970
The fourth rule. At $140,000, they stop telling you no.

131
00:08:34,370 --> 00:08:36,085
They just stop calling instead.

132
00:08:37,770 --> 00:08:41,291
Level five, $780,000.

133
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:45,141
An investor flies in. A small firm.

134
00:08:45,540 --> 00:08:52,587
They buy twenty percent of the company. Your share after taxes is $780,000,

135
00:08:52,650 --> 00:08:55,761
and it lands at 2:14 on a Wednesday afternoon.

136
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:59,953
You're in a coffee shop. Your latte costs $6.25.

137
00:09:00,350 --> 00:09:03,199
You stare at the receipt longer than the deposit.

138
00:09:03,990 --> 00:09:08,087
At this number, Dana has a manager, and the manager wants lunch.

139
00:09:08,490 --> 00:09:12,220
He orders for the table. He asks how old your parents are.

140
00:09:12,620 --> 00:09:14,610
He's very interested in planning.

141
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:19,219
You buy a house twenty minutes from Nana's. Four bedrooms.

142
00:09:19,620 --> 00:09:23,503
Sunday dinners start drifting your way. Nobody decides it.

143
00:09:23,900 --> 00:09:26,928
Nana's knees get worse, your kitchen is bigger,

144
00:09:26,990 --> 00:09:29,971
and one week The Porch just says, "At yours?"

145
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:34,963
At your table, Pete doesn't tell the story about you locking your keys

146
00:09:35,030 --> 00:09:36,836
in the car with the engine running.

147
00:09:37,240 --> 00:09:42,177
He used to tell it every year. Everybody laughed. You laughed loudest.

148
00:09:42,580 --> 00:09:46,070
This year he starts it, glances at you, and stops halfway.

149
00:09:46,470 --> 00:09:48,764
He turns it into a story about the weather.

150
00:09:49,550 --> 00:09:53,358
Jokes are the first thing to leave. You only tease equals.

151
00:09:54,150 --> 00:09:59,079
Renée brings her boyfriend. He shakes your hand like he's meeting a landlord.

152
00:09:59,480 --> 00:10:03,938
Your nephew, four years old, asks why your house has so many doors.

153
00:10:04,340 --> 00:10:05,914
Everyone laughs too hard.

154
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:10,088
After dinner, you find Nana's blue tin on your counter.

155
00:10:10,490 --> 00:10:14,628
She brought it from her house. You open it. Rubber bands.

156
00:10:15,030 --> 00:10:19,236
A spool of thread. Three twenties. You add ten more.

157
00:10:19,640 --> 00:10:20,631
You don't tell her.

158
00:10:21,420 --> 00:10:22,471
The fifth rule.

159
00:10:22,870 --> 00:10:28,564
At $780,000, they stop telling the stories where you look small.

160
00:10:28,960 --> 00:10:30,934
And those were your favorite ones.

161
00:10:32,620 --> 00:10:35,508
Level six, $3.2 million.

162
00:10:36,460 --> 00:10:41,132
The company sells a second stake. Bigger firm. Glass building.

163
00:10:41,530 --> 00:10:43,776
A pen that weighs more than your first phone.

164
00:10:44,180 --> 00:10:50,454
Your share is $3.2 million. You sign, ride the elevator down forty floors,

165
00:10:50,520 --> 00:10:54,504
and sit in the parking garage for twenty-two minutes with the radio off.

166
00:10:55,290 --> 00:10:59,584
Now the bank finds you. Dana is gone. There's a team.

167
00:10:59,980 --> 00:11:04,927
A relationship manager named Corinne, a tax person, an estate person,

168
00:11:04,990 --> 00:11:08,022
a person whose job you never fully understand.

169
00:11:08,420 --> 00:11:12,211
They call before you think to call them. They know your birthday.

170
00:11:12,610 --> 00:11:15,351
They send a cake. Who sends a cake?

171
00:11:16,140 --> 00:11:20,664
The Porch gets quieter. Fewer pings. Then you learn why.

172
00:11:21,060 --> 00:11:26,641
Renée mentions "the other chat" by accident. There's a second one. Same people.

173
00:11:27,040 --> 00:11:30,425
Minus you. Not cruel. Practical.

174
00:11:30,820 --> 00:11:34,175
Somewhere to talk about money without the person who has it.

175
00:11:34,960 --> 00:11:38,186
You ask your mother. She says, "Oh, that's nothing."

176
00:11:38,590 --> 00:11:41,791
Then, "We didn't want everything to feel like it was about you."

177
00:11:42,580 --> 00:11:48,067
Not exclusion. Courtesy. That's what makes it impossible to argue with.

178
00:11:48,860 --> 00:11:51,253
Pete pitches you a barbecue restaurant.

179
00:11:51,650 --> 00:11:56,832
He's been working on the menu for six years. He wants $75,000.

180
00:11:57,230 --> 00:12:01,146
You read it. It isn't good. And for the first time,

181
00:12:01,210 --> 00:12:04,779
the man who once called your ideas dumb sits across from you,

182
00:12:04,840 --> 00:12:07,529
waiting for you to call his dumb, and you can't.

183
00:12:07,930 --> 00:12:12,468
You write a check for $40,000 and a note that says good luck.

184
00:12:12,870 --> 00:12:15,612
He thanks you in a voice you don't recognize.

185
00:12:16,400 --> 00:12:21,941
The sixth rule. At $3.2 million, they stop telling you the truth.

186
00:12:22,340 --> 00:12:23,856
And you stop telling it back.

187
00:12:25,540 --> 00:12:28,028
Level seven, $18 million.

188
00:12:28,980 --> 00:12:31,107
The full sale. Everything.

189
00:12:31,510 --> 00:12:35,126
The software, the team, the office that smelled like steam.

190
00:12:35,530 --> 00:12:39,304
A larger company buys it in a deal that takes nine months and

191
00:12:39,370 --> 00:12:44,038
a hundred and forty pages. Your number is $18 million.

192
00:12:44,830 --> 00:12:46,538
Your father has been tired.

193
00:12:46,940 --> 00:12:50,440
Your mother mentions it once, in October, and never again.

194
00:12:50,840 --> 00:12:55,517
In December, he forgets your nephew's name at dinner. Just for a second.

195
00:12:55,920 --> 00:12:57,015
He laughs it off.

196
00:12:57,800 --> 00:13:03,081
You call a concierge medical practice. $12,000 a year to join.

197
00:13:03,480 --> 00:13:07,043
The receptionist calls back in nine minutes. Nine.

198
00:13:07,440 --> 00:13:11,986
You remember forty-one minutes of piano over a $35 fee.

199
00:13:12,390 --> 00:13:17,099
A neurologist sees your father that Thursday. Every scan money can buy.

200
00:13:17,500 --> 00:13:20,830
Every second opinion. Results in four days.

201
00:13:21,620 --> 00:13:22,993
Early-stage dementia.

202
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:26,804
The doctor is kind. She speaks slowly.

203
00:13:27,200 --> 00:13:30,870
She keeps a box of tissues on the desk at exactly the right

204
00:13:30,930 --> 00:13:37,307
angle. And there's nothing to buy. You look. You actually look. A trial.

205
00:13:37,710 --> 00:13:40,541
A clinic. A specialist overseas.

206
00:13:40,940 --> 00:13:43,478
There is no level where this one goes away.

207
00:13:44,270 --> 00:13:48,063
You find out later. He'd known something was wrong for a year.

208
00:13:48,460 --> 00:13:52,681
He told your mother. He told Pete. He told Nana.

209
00:13:53,080 --> 00:13:54,188
He didn't tell you.

210
00:13:54,980 --> 00:13:56,558
When you finally ask why,

211
00:13:56,620 --> 00:13:59,757
he sits on your porch in the good chair you bought him and

212
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:01,490
says, "You'd have tried to fix it."

213
00:14:02,280 --> 00:14:07,063
Not secrecy. Mercy. He wanted one problem you couldn't own.

214
00:14:07,850 --> 00:14:10,805
Then he looks out at the yard and says, "We're fine."

215
00:14:11,590 --> 00:14:17,131
The seventh rule. At $18 million, they stop telling you when they're scared.

216
00:14:17,530 --> 00:14:19,394
Because your fear costs too much.

217
00:14:21,080 --> 00:14:23,286
Level eight, $60 million.

218
00:14:24,230 --> 00:14:30,907
The money grows without you now. You don't work. It works. Funds. Buildings.

219
00:14:31,310 --> 00:14:35,483
Stakes in things you've never seen. People ask you to sit on boards.

220
00:14:35,880 --> 00:14:38,849
You say yes to one. A regional bank.

221
00:14:39,250 --> 00:14:41,875
Quarterly meetings in a room with no clocks.

222
00:14:42,660 --> 00:14:46,563
At your third meeting, a slide goes up. Fee revenue.

223
00:14:46,960 --> 00:14:55,986
One line reads, "Overdraft, $35 per occurrence." Projected annual income, $212 million.

224
00:14:56,390 --> 00:14:59,090
Someone asks if the number is defensible.

225
00:14:59,490 --> 00:15:04,119
Someone else says customers value the convenience. Everyone nods.

226
00:15:04,520 --> 00:15:07,781
A woman refills your sparkling water before you ask.

227
00:15:08,570 --> 00:15:14,530
You think about $3.79 of detergent. A Tuesday. 11:40 at night.

228
00:15:15,320 --> 00:15:17,735
You vote. You vote with the room.

229
00:15:18,520 --> 00:15:21,417
You fund a memory care wing at the county hospital.

230
00:15:21,820 --> 00:15:24,710
They put your family name on the wall in brushed steel.

231
00:15:25,110 --> 00:15:28,533
Your father stands in front of it in a shirt your mother ironed,

232
00:15:28,600 --> 00:15:32,302
and doesn't quite know why he's there. He knows it's important.

233
00:15:32,700 --> 00:15:34,945
He can tell by how people are acting.

234
00:15:35,730 --> 00:15:39,776
At Sunday dinner, everyone is pleasant. Everyone is fine.

235
00:15:40,180 --> 00:15:42,558
Renée tells you nothing about her marriage,

236
00:15:42,620 --> 00:15:45,710
which is coming apart, which you learn from your nephew.

237
00:15:46,110 --> 00:15:49,768
Pete's restaurant closed in March. He never mentioned it.

238
00:15:50,170 --> 00:15:54,693
In The Porch, you get birthday wishes and baby photos and nothing else.

239
00:15:55,480 --> 00:15:59,284
After everyone leaves, you find the blue tin on your counter.

240
00:15:59,680 --> 00:16:04,470
Nana's been gone two winters. Somebody keeps bringing it to your house anyway.

241
00:16:04,870 --> 00:16:09,658
You open it. Your twenties. All of them. Untouched.

242
00:16:10,060 --> 00:16:14,367
And a strip of paper in your mother's handwriting. "Nobody needed it."

243
00:16:15,160 --> 00:16:18,685
You stand in your kitchen and hold the tin for a long time.

244
00:16:19,470 --> 00:16:20,499
The eighth rule.

245
00:16:20,900 --> 00:16:26,073
At $60 million, your family stops telling you anything you could change.

246
00:16:26,470 --> 00:16:28,265
Which turns out to be everything.

247
00:16:29,950 --> 00:16:34,705
Somewhere right now, someone is standing in a corner store at 11:40 on

248
00:16:34,770 --> 00:16:40,943
a Tuesday night, holding a $3.79 bottle of detergent. The card reader beeps.

249
00:16:41,340 --> 00:16:47,527
Approved. Tomorrow there'll be a $35 fee, and forty-one minutes of piano,

250
00:16:47,590 --> 00:16:49,426
and a voice like a parking meter.

251
00:16:49,830 --> 00:16:53,250
On Sunday, a screen door will bang twice behind them.

252
00:16:53,650 --> 00:16:58,029
Everyone at that table will tell them exactly what they're doing wrong.

253
00:16:58,430 --> 00:16:59,601
And on the way out,

254
00:16:59,660 --> 00:17:03,853
a grandmother will slip two folded twenties into their jacket pocket.

255
00:17:04,640 --> 00:17:08,145
They think the worst part is how much everyone tells them.

256
00:17:08,930 --> 00:17:12,397
It isn't. The worst part is the day it stops.

257
00:17:13,190 --> 00:17:15,989
Everyone's fine. They keep saying so.

